# _common.rules
# Applied to every PR regardless of area, in addition to the house
# engineering standards loaded for the PR's areas.
#
# SCOPE — read this before adding anything here.
#
# The authoritative definition of how code should be structured is the
# house engineering standards (the ql-docs stage checklists), loaded
# per area as `<area>.standards`. This file deliberately does NOT restate
# them: two definitions of the same requirement can drift apart, and the
# reviewer would then be citing whichever it happened to read first.
#
# What belongs here is only what no stage checklist covers — properties of
# the *pull request itself* rather than of the architecture: secrets in a
# diff, commit hygiene, suppressed checks, dependency provenance.
#
# If you are about to add an architectural, layering, naming, testing, or
# framework rule here: it belongs in the standards repository instead.
version: 2

## MUST
- No secrets, API keys, tokens, passwords, or private keys anywhere in the diff.
- No commented-out code blocks left in the diff.
- No TODO/FIXME comments without a linked task or issue reference.
- The PR's conventional-commit header matches the area(s) it actually touches.

## SHOULD
- The PR description explains the "why", not just the "what".
- Commits are logically scoped (one concern per commit) rather than one giant diff.
- Anything the author knows is incomplete or deliberately out of scope is stated in the PR description rather than left for the reviewer to discover.

## SECURITY
- No disabling of linters, type checks, or tests via inline suppressions (`eslint-disable`, `// @ts-ignore`, `.skip`, `@SuppressWarnings`, etc.) without a one-line justification comment.
- No test weakened, skipped, or deleted in order to make a build pass; a failing test is either a real defect or a wrong expectation, and both need a deliberate decision.
- No new dependency added without a corresponding lockfile change.
